qx|A2|st||rh||ah|Board 2 IMPs   -   April 3, 2024|pn|hondo717,ladydoc,Honeydog3,aspiring|
md|4SQJ5HJTD62CAK9753,SATHKQ9652DAK8CQ4,S864HDJT9753CJ862,SK9732HA8743DQ4CT|sv|n|
mb|P|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Sometimes it is right to preempt 3@C with a 6-card suit. Let me discuss when I do it. In 1st seat, I will do it if I have an unbalanced hand (e.g. singleton or void) and a good suit, especially if not vulnerable. Even in 1st seat I would not open 3@C with my hand.|
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In 2nd seat I virtually never open 3@C with a 6-card suit, especially vulnerable. Although you should definitely preempt in 2nd seat, it is the most risky seat in which to preempt. The reason being since one of the opponents already passed, it is much more likely partner is strong and you will preempt them.|
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In 2nd seat I tend to be relatively disciplined when preempting.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


At|^^
^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*NWhy not open 1@C?|
PG| ***** 25 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I could but @HJ is dubious.|
PG| ***** 26 ***** |


mb|1H|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|4C|an|singleton or void|
pg| ***** 27 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I like East's 4@C |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=splinters,Splinter^*N|CP||FP||at|, particularly since she passed originally. Had East not passed I am not sure what I would respond to 1@H. Definitely not 4@H.|

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 28 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let's study what East should bid over 1@H had she not been a passed hand. East's has 9 HCP, less 1 for dubious @DQ, plus 2 length points, plus 1 point for doubleton, and plus 3 points for singleton with 4+ card trump support for 14 |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=dummypoints,Dummy Points^*N|CP||FP||at|. |

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 29 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|So clearly East can bid a 4@C bid over 1@H even if it was not a passed hand.|
PG| ***** 30 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Another possibility which I use sometimes with a limit raise with 5-card support is to make a |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenraises,Bergen Raises^*N|CP||FP||at| (3@C if playing Reverse Bergen to show 10-12). However, East has a hand too good for a Bergen limit raise.|

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 31 ***** |


mb|D|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West hand has 18 HCP, while I completely discount the @CQ because of the splinter, plus 2 length points, plus 1 point for 2 doubleton, and another point for the 6th heart once a heart fit is established for around 20 |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=declarerpoints,Bergen Declarer Points^*N|CP||FP||at|.|

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 32 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|With strength, I recommend West bidding RKCB (4@S). And because we will play |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=kickback,Kickback^*N|CP||FP||at| 4@S is RKCB when hearts are trump. |
PG| ***** 33 ***** |


mb|4S|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|5C|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As you note in this cycle, ^*B(5@C shows 1 (or 4) (2nd step)^*n, the advantage of |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=kickback,Kickback^*N|CP||FP||at| is it allows you to do the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=queenask,Queen Ask^*N|CP||FP||at| when partner has the 2nd step response.|
PG| ***** 34 ***** |


mb|P|mb|6H|mb|P|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|
pg| ***** 35 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|This is a great example of why |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenhandevaluation,Bergen Hand Evaluation^*N|CP||FP||at| is so good. EW only have 27 HCP, yet slam is just about cold. And even without the @CQ (meaning they would have 25 HCP) slam is cold.|
PG| ***** 36 ***** |


pc|C8|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I am not sure why partner led the @C8. The @C2 is the standard lead. But, I let it stand.|
